Synergistic effect   
4-aminobenzoic acid ethyl ester
Manganese-metal organic frameworks (MOFs) derived manganese-carbon $(\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C})$ composites have rarely been synthesized for peroxymonosulfate (PMS) activation, and the complicated synergistic effect in this system are also unclear. In this study, 2D manganese-1,4 benzenedicarboxylic acid-based MOFs (Mn-MOFs) were calcined under different temperatures to obtain a series of nanosheet structured manganese-carbon $(\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C})$ composites on PMS activation for 4-aminobenzoic acid ethyl ester (ABEE) degradation. Owing to the rational coordination of specific surface area $(268.42~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ and mesoporous structure, higher $\equiv\mathtt{M n}(\mathrm{II})$ content, more carbonyl groups and defective sites, $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ exhibited excellent ABEE adsorption $(27.0~\%$ and degrada­ tion performance $(91.3\%$ within $30\mathrm{min}\dot{}$ ) and recycling ability (the removal efficiency of ABEE only dropped by $4.7~\%$ after four consecutive recovery tests). Singlet oxygen $(^{1}0_{2})$ , superoxide radical $(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-})$ , hydroxyl radical ( OH), sulfate radical $({\cal S}{\cal O}_{4}{}^{\bullet-})$ , and surface electron transfer process were all involved in ABEE degradation. Among them, $^10_{2}$ was the main reactive oxygen species (ROS). Besides, the different functions of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathbf{x}}$ and carbon component were studied to confirm their synergistic effect. The carbon component could be complexed with PMS and adsorb ABEE on the catalyst surface, which could facilitate the reaction between $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}$ and PMS, as well as ABEE and the generated ROS. Meanwhile, the evenly distributed $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathbf{x}}$ could improve the electron transfer capability of the carbon component. This study introduced an in-situ synthesis method of $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C}$ composites, and provided an in-depth understanding of manganese-carbon interaction mechanisms in PMS activation, which might prompt the development of other MOFs-derived metal–carbon hybrid materials for environmental remediation.
## 1. Introduction
Pharmaceutical and personal care products (PPCPs), including pre­ scription and non-prescription drugs, cosmetics, fragrances, biological and health products, have attracted increasing attention due to their widespread applications and unrestricted discharge [1,2]. As one of the typical PPCPs, 4-aminobenzoic acid ethyl ester (ABEE) is widely used in sunscreens and anesthetic salves, up to a content of $20\%$ [3]. Although ABEE is water soluble $(958~\mathrm{mg~L^{-1}}$ , $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ ) and is widely used, its envi­ ronmental behavior and pollution control have hardly been reported. Adela Jing Lia et al sampled and measured 30 locations near the coast of Hong Kong, but ABEE was not detected in any of them [3]. This is because ABEE can be transformed into various transformation products (TPs) under light irradiation. These TPs have been proved to have luminescence inhibitory effects on the marine bacterium $V.$ fischeri. In the study of photocatalytic degradation of ABEE, Jiang et al found that both ABEE and its macromolecular intermediate products were acutely toxic to Daphnia magna $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\cdot D}$ . magna), $\mathrm{LC}_{50}$ was about $1.186~\mathrm{mg~L}^{-1}$ and $0.362\mathrm{~mg~L}^{-1}$ , respectively [2]. Therefore, it is very meaningful to degrade ABEE and its TPs into smaller molecules or achieve complete mineralization.
Advanced oxidation processes (AOPs) are considered to be capable of eliminating a wide range of pollutants, especially those recalcitrant organic pollutants, to innocuous or low toxic small compounds, or even complete mineralization to $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ [4,5]. Compared with hydroxyl radical ( OH)-generating Fenton reaction, sulfate radical $({\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}^{\bullet-\cdot})$ -based
AOPs (SRAOPs) with higher redox potential $(2.5\substack{-3.1\mathrm{V}}$ vs $1.9{-}2.7\mathrm{~V~}$ of OH), longer half-life $(30-40~{\upmu\mathrm{s}})$ , wider operating $\mathsf{p H}$ (2–8) and higher selectivity for organics that contain unsaturated bonds or aromatic $\pi$ electrons have been deemed as a promising alternative [5–8]. Perox­ ymonosulfate (PMS, $\mathrm{HSO}_{5}^{-})$ with an unsymmetrical structure is a major source for SRAOPs. Activation of PMS by transition metal ions including ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ , $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}$ and relevant heterogeneous catalysts have been increasingly published [5,9]. Among them, ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ exhibited the best efficiency for PMS activation [10]. Various heterogeneous cobalt-based catalysts have been explored to reduce the loss of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ [11–14]. How­ ever, the leaching of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ still exists and is a great concern for human beings’ health. Hence, study direction towards catalysts with environ­ mental friendliness is preferred.
Manganese oxides $(\mathrm{MnO_{x})}$ with low toxicity and high abundance in soil have been investigated since the first paper used $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/M n O_{2}}$ for PMS activation in 2012 [4,15]. $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathbf{x}}$ was demonstrated to activate PMS mainly through one-electron transfer because they have brilliant oxygen mobility, which can realize the transition of different valence states of Mn. However, Mn-based materials still have some drawbacks, including low dispersity, poor electrical conductivity and limited organic affinity [16]. To overcome these problems, various $\mathtt{M n O_{x}}$ -carbonaceous hybrids have been considered in recent years. The combination of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathbf{x}}$ and carbonaceous materials not only have more active sites, higher stability and better conductivity, but also the oxygen functional groups of carbon materials such as ketonic carbonyl groups $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O})$ and defects such as vacancies and zigzag edges could activate PMS for reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation or direct electron abstraction of pollutants [17,18]. Most manganese/carbon composites were synthesized by the post-synthetic modification strategies [16,19,20], involving complex preparation processes. Thus, the facile pyrolysis of Mn-based metal organic frameworks (Mn-MOFs) under an inert atmosphere seems to be a promising strategy. Furthermore, the MOFs-derived carbon materials could inherit excellent properties such as high specific BET, plentiful and uniform active sites [21]. Li et al successfully prepared iron/carbon composites by in-situ carbonization of MIL-88A(Fe) as effective PS ac­ tivators for phenol degradation [17]. Nevertheless, current research mainly focused on Fe- or Co– related MOFs [8,22–24], derivatives of Mn-MOFs for PMS activation have been rarely reported. Therefore, direct pyrolysis of Mn-MOFs for PMS activation should be studied, and the activation mechanisms need further investigation.
Herein, novel manganese-carbon $(\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C})$ composites derived from 2D manganese-1,4 benzenedicarboxylic acid-based MOFs (MnMOFs) were synthesized for PMS activation and ABEE degradation. Firstly, the effect of pyrolysis temperatures on the chemical composi­ tion, morphology, structures, surface chemical properties, etc. of $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C s}$ were studied to explore the phase conversion process under argon (Ar) atmosphere. Then, catalytic performance and stability of $\operatorname{MnO}_{\mathrm{x}}@\operatorname{Cs}$ were evaluated. Combined with a series of ABEE degradation tests, recovery characterizations, chemical probe experiments, electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) tests and electrochemical measurements, the different mechanisms involved in PMS activation were proposed and demonstrated. Meanwhile, the catalytic synergism effect between $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathbf{x}}$ and carbon component was revealed. Finally, the intermediate products of ABEE were analyzed to find out the degradation pathway of ABEE.
## 2. Materials and experimental procedure
## 2.1. Chemicals
Manganese nitrate $(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2})$ was obtained from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Technology Co., LTD. Oxone $(2\mathrm{KHSO}_{5}{\bullet\mathrm{KHSO}_{4}{\bullet\mathrm{K}_{2}S O_{4}}})$ was purchased from Saen Chemical Co., China. 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}B D C)}$ was purchased from Beijing Beiluo Biotechnology Co., LTD. 4- aminobenzoic acid ethyl ester (ABEE) was provided by Shanghai Chembee Fine Chemicals Co., LTD. N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF) and methanol $\mathrm{(CH_{3}O H)}$ were obtained from Sinopharm Chemical ReagentCo., Ltd. All chemicals were used as received without further purification.
## 2.2. Synthesis of MnOx@C
Mn-MOF was synthesized via a facile solvothermal method based on the previous reports [25,26]. $\mathtt{M n O_{X}@C}$ samples were obtained by the subsequent carbonization. Typically, 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}B D C)}$ $\left(1.242\ \mathrm{mmol}\right)$ and $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ $\left(1.242\ \mathrm{mmol}\right)$ was dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ and 30 mL N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF) under ultrasound for $10\mathrm{min}$ , respectively. Next, the above solutions were mixed with ultra­ sonication for $5\mathrm{min}$ and then stirred vigorously for $30\mathrm{min}$ . The derived $70~\mathrm{mL}$ solution was then transferred to $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ Teflon-lined stainlesssteel autoclaves and heated at $140^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After the reaction, MnMOF was obtained by centrifugation, washed with DMF and MeOH three times respectively, and finally dried in vacuum to remove MeOH. The obtained powders after grinding were annealed under Ar atmo­ sphere at $400^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , $500^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , $600^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , $700^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , $800^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , $900^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $1000^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ with a ramp rate of $5~{\mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}}$ to synthesize $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C}$ samples.
## 2.3. Characterization
Details of characterization methods were described in Text S1
## 2.4. Catalytic degradation experiments
ABEE was selected as the target pollutant to test the Fenton perfor­ mance of obtained $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C}$ catalysts. The degradation experiment was conducted in a $250~\mathrm{mL}$ conical flask containing $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ ABEE solution $(10\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ with $50\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ catalyst and $0.15\ \mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ PMS at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ without pH adjustment, and $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ HCl or NaOH were used to adjust the pH values if needed. Typically, the solution with catalyst was vibrated for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ to achieve adsorption–desorption equipoise, then PMS was added to initiate the reaction. At each time interval, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ reaction so­ lution sample was collected and instantly quenched by $0.5\mathrm{mL}$ methanol, then it was injected into a $2.0~\mathrm{mL}$ high-performance liquid chromatog­ raphy (HPLC) vial by Millipore film $\cdot220\ \mathrm{nm})$ for analysis. For the recycling test, the used catalyst was recovered through centrifugation, rinsed with deionized water and MeOH three times, and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for the next run.
## 2.5. Analysis
Concentrations of ABEE were obtained by HPLC (LC-2010AHT, Shimadzu) equipped with a UV detector and an Agilent ZORBAX SB-C18 column. Detailed detection parameters could be found in Table S1. TOC analyzer (TOC-Control V, Shimadzu) was used to analyze the total organic carbon (TOC) content of the solution sample. The determination of ABEE degradation intermediates were performed on a liquid chromatography-mass spectrometer (LC-MS, Agilent 1290 UPLC/6550 QTOF). Detailed detection parameters could be found in Table S2.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of $M n O_{x}@C s$
Thermal pyrolysis $(30-1000^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ under Ar atmosphere was completed to investigate the carbonization property of Mn-MOF. As shown in the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) curve (Fig. 1a), the procedure con­ tains five weight-loss stages. The weight loss $(18.47\%)$ ) below $150^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ at the first step was due to the removal of adsorbed or crystalline water. Then DMF molecules were removed and the organic content started to decompose at the second step $(5.83~\%)$ from 150 to $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The third step between 450 and $545~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ with a sharp weight loss $(31.85~\%)$
indicated the collapse of structure and the intense decomposition of $\mathsf{B D C}^{2\cdot}$ ligands. The following soft mass loss $(5.27\%)$ at the fourth stage $(545-870~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ can be attributed to the generation of carbon skeleton. After that, the last stage with an apparent weight loss $(8.25~\%)$ ) showed the further graphitization of the carbon structure.
The XRD patterns for $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C s}$ pyrolyzed at different temperatures were measured. As presented in Fig. S1, the characteristic peak of MnMOF at $9.4~^{\circ}$ was still maintained in $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot400$ , and some new diffraction peaks appeared, illustrating a small degree of pyrolysis. As shown in Fig. 1b, cubic MnO (JCPDS No. 75–1090) at 2θ of $34.9^{\circ}$ , $40.5^{\circ}$ , $58.6^{\circ}$ , $70.2^{\circ}$ , $73.8^{\circ}$ and tetragonal-phase $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (JCPDS No. 24–0734) at 2θ of $17.9^{\circ}$ , $28.9^{\circ}$ , $32.3^{\circ}$ , $36.0^{\circ}$ , $59.7^{\circ}$ can be found in all $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C}$ samples, implying that the structure of Mn-MOF had been totally des­ tructed at $500^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . Moreover, $\mathbf{MnO}$ was prominent in these $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C s}$ and the peak intensity of MnO strengthened as annealing temperature increased, suggesting the better crystallinity. For $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C-1000}$ , peaks of $\mathbf{Mn}_{7}\mathbf{C}_{3}$ (JCPDS No. 80–1699) at $39.6^{\mathrm{~\circ~}}$ , $42.5^{\mathrm{~\circ~}}$ , $44.6~^{\circ}$ , $50.3~^{\circ}$ , $79.1^{\circ}$ and (002) plane of graphitic carbon at $26.2~^{\circ}$ were observed, demon­ strating the production of $\mathbf{Mn}_{7}\mathbf{C}_{3}$ and graphitization of amorphous car­ bon at high temperatures. Notably, graphitic carbon was only observed in $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C-1000}$ , which may be due to the amorphous carbon accounted for the main part when the calcination temperature was below $1000^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ .
The surface functional groups of Mn-MOF and $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C s}$ were also analyzed by FTIR (Fig. S5). The stretching vibration of $-\mathrm{OH}$ at $2500{-}3000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was not observed in $\mathbf{Mn-MOF}$ , indicating that the carboxyl groups (–COOH) in $\mathrm{{H_{2}B D C}}$ were all bound to the $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ . The two C-O bonds of –COO- had comparable vibration frequency and strong vibration coupling effect, causing two new absorption peaks at 1558 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and $1395~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [39]. After carbonization, $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C s}$ showed a slight difference from Mn-MOF, stretching vibration of $0{\mathrm{-H}}$ at 3442 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and Mn-O at $503\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ maintained, while $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ at $1730\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $\mathtt{C=C}$ and adsorbed water at $1625\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ emerged. Besides, from $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}{\cdot}500$ to $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}{\cdot}700$ , carboxylic C-O at $1260~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ appeared, which was consistent with the XPS results [29,40]. As the calcination temperature increased, the peaks of carboxylic C-O weakened gradually, implying the decomposition of surface oxygen-containing functional groups. Raman spectra of $\operatorname{MnO}_{\mathrm{x}}@\operatorname{Cs}$ were also investigated (Fig. S6). $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C-}$ 700, $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ and $\mathtt{M n O_{x}}@\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-}1000$ displayed D band $(1350~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}).$ ) and G band $(1600~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1})}$ corresponding to the defects and distortion in carbon layer and graphitization extent, respectively [41]. However, the D peak and G peak were not observed in the spectrum of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}{\cdot}500$ , which confirmed that the decomposition of $\mathsf{B D C}^{2-}$ ligands and the for­ mation of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathbf{x}}$ occurred at this stage, and Mn-MOF had not been carbonized or the degree of carbonization was extremely low. The $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}{\cdot}700$ , $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ and $\mathtt{M n O_{x}}@\mathtt{C}\cdot1000$ were 0.79, 0.89, 0.95 and 0.73, respectively, indicating that the defect degree significantly increased from $700^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to $900^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . As the pyrolysis temper­ ature increased to $1000^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , the defects were eliminated and the graph­ itization degree raised.
The morphological characteristics of Mn-MOF and $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C s}$ were shown in SEM images (Fig. 3). As seen from Fig. 3a, Mn-MOF presented a bulky plate structure with a smooth surface, which agreed well with the reported literature [42]. After thermal treatment at $500^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , $700^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , 900 $^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $1000\ {^{\circ}}\mathsf{C}$ , Fig. 3(b-f) showed that the pyrolysis products still maintained 2D structure, except the smaller thickness and rougher surface. Many pore structures were formed on the surface of all $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C s}$ due to the release of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and a large number of regular shaped nanoparticles were embedded in and on $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ and $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C-1000}$ . These nanoparticles were confirmed to be $\mathtt{M n O_{x}}$ by XRD (Fig. 1b) and EDS (Fig. 4c), indicating the formation of crystalline manganese phases under high calcination temperature. The uniform embedded manganese oxides in $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C}{\cdot}900$ (Fig. 3e) showed that the highly ordered manganese ions in the prepared Mn-MOF were regularly separated by $\mathsf{B D C}^{2-}$ ligands, thereby effectively preventing the serious accumulation of manganese oxides during the pyrolysis process. How­ ever, the hard/soft acid/base (HSAB) theory shows that coordination bonds between hard bases and soft Lewis acids are not stable enough [43]. Therefore, the intense decomposition of $\mathsf{B D C}^{2-}$ ligands (hard bases) during the pyrolysis process led to some aggregated $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ particles on the surface of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ . When the Mn-MOF was py­ rolyzed above $900^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , Fig. 3f clearly showed that the nanosheet struc­ ture of $\mathtt{M n O_{x}}@\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-}1000$ was ruined to a certain extent, one reason is that the high temperature caused the collapse of the carbon framework, the other is the non-quantitative interstitial compound $\mathbf{Mn}_{7}\mathbf{C}_{3}$ formed by carbon atoms entering the manganese lattice as interstitial atoms [44].
The microstructure of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ was investigated by TEM and HRTEM (Fig. 4). As shown in Fig. 4(a-c), $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ presented a 2D sheet-like morphology with numerous manganese oxides nanoparticles evenly embedded in the composites and a small amount were on the surface. It can also be seen that particles embedded in the carbon frame had smaller particle sizes and better dispersibility than those on the surface, indicating that the carbon framework can effectively avoid the agglomeration of manganese oxides. By measuring the lattice spacings (Fig. 4(d-f)), $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ had a lattice fringe of $0.255\ \mathrm{nm}$ , corre­ sponding to the (111) plane of MnO, and the $d$ -spacing values of 0.309, 0.238 and $0.270\ \mathrm{nm}$ fitted well with the crystal planes of (1 1 2), (0 0 4) and (103) of $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , further proving the existence of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathbf{x}}$ with different valence states.
Composites with a large specific area (SSA) and reasonable pore size distribution tend to perform well in the adsorption and catalysis of heterogeneous reactions. As displayed in Fig. S7, all $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C s}$ dis­ played typical type-IV isotherm with $\mathrm{H}_{3}$ hysteresis loop, and pore di­ ameters were mostly between 2 and $10~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ , demonstrating the mesoporous structure of these composites. The textural parameters were listed in Table S3. Among them, $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}{\cdot}500$ had the largest pore volume $(0.47\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ and average pore size $(7.26\mathrm{nm})$ , owing to its loose structure formed by the intense decomposition of $\mathsf{B D C}^{2\cdot}$ ligands. When pyrolysis temperature increased to $700^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , the formation of the nano­ sheet structure made $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot700$ have a larger $\boldsymbol{S}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ $(270.86~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ and smaller pore volume $(0.26\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ and average pore size $(3.79\mathrm{nm})$ . For $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ , high temperature led to a more porous structure, the SBET was almost unchanged and the pore volume $(0.30~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ and average pore size $(4.45\ \mathrm{nm})$ further increased. However, the excessive temperature would cause the collapse of the carbon skeleton, resulting in a smaller $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ $(175.44~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ and pore volume $(0.28~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ .
## 3.2. Evaluation of catalytic performance
## 3.2.1. Catalytic activity of $M n O_{x}@C s$
$\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C s}$ obtained at different temperatures were used to activate PMS for ABEE degradation. Since previous studies proved that the adsorption property of the catalyst has an important impact on catalytic efficiency, $30~\mathrm{min}$ adsorption and desorption process was set before adding PMS. As shown in Fig. 5a, as the calcination temperature increased from $500^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to $900^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , the adsorption removal efficiency of ABEE increased from $4.1\ \%$ to $27.0~\%$ . However, $\mathtt{M n O_{x}}@\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-}1000$ only adsorbed $10.8\%$ ABEE. This trend was basically consistent with the law of $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ and pore volume. Although the $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ was slightly smaller than that of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot700$ , its pore volume and average pore size were larger than $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}{\cdot}700$ , demonstrating that the rational com­ bination of $\ensuremath{s}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ and porosity determines the adsorption performance of composites.
In degradation experiments, $49.0\%$ of ABEE were degraded by PMS alone in $60\mathrm{min}$ , demonstrating that the self-decomposition of PMS can oxidize a certain amount of ABEE. A similar conclusion had been re­ ported before [21]. The degradation efficiency of ABEE $(10\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ by $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ $(0.05\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1})$ and PMS $(0.15\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1})$ in $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ was $91.3~\%$ , which was higher than $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}{\cdot}500$ $(69.2\%)$ , $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}{\cdot}600$ $(80.3\%)$ , $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C}{\cdot}700$ $(84.4~\%)$ , $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}{\cdot}800$ $(87.0~\%)$ and $\mathtt{M n O_{x}}\ @\mathtt{C}\cdot1000$ $(83.2\%)$ . Besides, $67.7\%$ of ABEE could be removed by $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C}{\cdot}900/$ PMS in 5 min and $36.5\%$ TOC (Fig. S8) was removed within $30\mathrm{min}$ . The reaction rates were calculated by the pseudo-first-order kinetics model (Fig. S9a) and pseudo-second-order kinetics model (Fig. S9b), respec­ tively. The regression coefficients $(\mathsf{R}^{2})$ indicated that the degradation of ABEE better obeyed the pseudo-second-order kinetics model. Thus, the rate constants under different reaction parameters were calculated by the pseudo-second-order kinetics model. As shown in Fig. 5b, $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C-}$ 900 showed the highest $k$ value $(32.3\mathrm{~L~g}^{-1}\mathrm{~min}^{-1})$ among $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C s}$ . Combined with the physical and chemical properties of $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C s}$ , the excellent catalytic activity of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ could be due to the highest content of Mn (II) and carbon active sites (carbonyl group and defective sites) and the best adsorption effect. Besides, the synergistic effect of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ and carbon in the catalytic process can not be ignored.
3.2.2. Effects of $M n O_{x}@C\ –900$ loading, PMS dosage and solution $p H$ The effects of several reaction conditions on ABEE degradation ef­ ficiency were conducted, as shown in Fig. 6.
First, Fig. 6a showed that as the amount of the catalyst increased from $0.05\mathrm{gL}^{-1}$ to $0.2\ \mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , the adsorption capacity (from $28.2\%$ to 53.1 $\%$ and degradation rate (from $32.3\mathrm{\bar{L}g^{-1}\ m i n^{-1}}$ to $641.0\mathrm{L}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{min}^{-1})$ both increased significantly, mainly due to the introduction of more adsorption and active sites. The result of Fig. 6b also accorded with this conclusion. It can be seen that the increase in PMS dosage (from $0.15{\mathrm{gL}}^{-}$ 1 to $0.50\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ enhanced the removal efficiency (from $91.4\%$ to $98.6\%$ ) and rate $(32.3\mathrm{~L~g}^{-1}\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$ to $321.0\mathrm{~L~g}^{-1}\mathrm{~min}^{-1};$ . Although PMS was able to oxidize a certain amount of PMS, the TOC removal ratio was only $2.41\ \%$ , which was much less than $39.46~\%$ of $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C}{\cdot}900/\mathrm{PMS}$ sys­ tem, indicating that PMS could only simply degrade ABEE into inter­ mediate products.
