## 3.2.3. Effects of inorganic anions and NOMs
In actual water bodies, there are various inorganic anions and organic substances, which can react with radicals and affect the removal efficiency of ABEE. Therefore, $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{HCO_{3}}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}{}^{-}$ and humic acid (HA) were introduced into the $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C}.900/\mathrm{PMS}$ system to evaluate the influence of water matrix on ABEE decomposition.
Fig. 7a presented that $1\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ showed slight inhibition on ABEE degradation (from $91.3\%$ to $88.3\%$ ). The existence of $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ could quench $g_{0_{4}}\bullet_{\mathbf{\tau}}$ $\left(\mathrm{E}^{0}=2.5-3.1\ \mathrm{V}\right)$ to form chlorine radicals (Cl•, $\mathrm{E}^{0}=2.40\mathrm{~V~}$ ; $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ , $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{E}}^{0}=2.09\:\boldsymbol{\mathrm{V}}$ regardless of the pH value (Eqs. $(3)-(4))$ [24]. The lower oxidation potential of chlorine radicals was not conducive to the degradation of ABEE. However, when the concentration of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ increased to $5\mathrm{mM}$ and $10\mathrm{mM}$ , the removal ratio of ABEE increased to $96.6\%$ and $100\%$ . According to the previous research, PMS could oxidize $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ to generate active chlorines including $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ and HOCl (Eqs. (5) – (6)) to enhance the removal of pollutants [46,47]. To find out whether the enhancement of PMS activation by high concentration of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ is related to the catalyst, the TOC removal ratios of ABEE in PMS only, $\mathrm{Cl^{-}/P M S}$ , $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C}.900/\mathrm{PMS}$ and $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C}{\cdot}900/\mathrm{Cl}^{-}/\mathrm{PMS}$ systems were investi­ gated. It can be seen in Fig. S11 that when $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}{\cdot}900$ did not exist, $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ could increase the TOC removal ratio from $2.41~\%$ to $9.25~\%$ . However, when $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ was given, the addition of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ signifi­ cantly increased the TOC removal ratio of ABEE (from $39.46\%$ to 70.87 $\%$ . A similar phenomenon has been reported before [48]. Several studies showed that when PMS was not activated, no Cl• was generated and the production rates of active chlorines were very low when compared to the rates obtained when a radical process takes place [49,50]. Therefore, the more and faster production of active chlorine species could react with ABEE and offset the scavenging of $g_{0_{4}}\bullet_{\mathbf{\phi}}$ by $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ (Eqs. (7) – (9)) [51]. However, the addition of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ might cause the production of chlorinated derivatives, such as trichloromethane and carbon tetrachloride, which are undesirable in environment and difficult to be oxidized [49].
