ABEE degradation was also conducted with the addition of HA (Fig. 7d). Although HA occupied some adsorption sites, the removal ratio of ABEE only dropped by $2.4~\%$ at most. This might be because while HA were adsorbed on the surface of the catalyst to block the re­ action sites, the semiquinone radicals formed by hydroquinones, qui­ nones and phenols in HA could produce $s0_{4}^{~\bullet-}$ by reducing PMS [56,57]. This activation mechanism could reduce the negative impact of the occupied active sites in the $\mathrm{MnO_{x}@C-900/P M S/A B E}$ E system to a certain extent, thus endowing the system with excellent anti-NOMs interference performance when used to treat ABEE.
## 3.3. Recyclability and stability of MnOx@C-900
Four cycle experiments were conducted to evaluate the durability of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ in PMS activation (Fig. S12). The removal efficiency decreased slightly after each run, and the removal ratio of ABEE decreased from $90.8~\%$ to $86.1\ \%$ after four consecutive recovery tests. The decreased activity of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ might be due to the coverage and loss of active sites. To further prove that $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ has the potential to be applied to treat environmental water, tap water from the laboratory and secondary effluent of wastewater treatment plant were used to replace deionized water to assess the catalytic activity of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ . As shown in Fig. S13, the degradation efficiency of ABEE in tap water was basically unaffected and only decreased by $12.3~\%$ in treated wastewater, indicating that the catalytic performance of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ was quite stable. But it is worth noting that the adsorption efficiency of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}{\cdot}900$ decreased in the order of ultrapure water, tap water and secondary water. This might be due to the complex back­ ground of tap water and secondary effluent. The increased concentration of interfering ions and organics would compete with ABEE for adsorp­ tion sites, resulting in the continued decrease of adsorption performance of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ [58]. In this study, the catalyst was only washed with methanol and ultrapure water, no other steps were required. Simple recovery method and stable removal efficiency in actual water bodies proved that $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ is very promising in water pollution application.
To further explore the reason for the decreased activity, the amount of leached manganese ions was tested by ICP-OES. The result showed that the leaching amount of manganese ions was $3.08~\mathrm{mg~L}^{-1}$ after re­ action. Besides, the relatively high concentration might be caused by the loss of agglomerated manganese oxide particles on the surface of the catalyst [59]. This can be observed from the comparison of SEM images (Fig. S14). After reaction, $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ still presented nanosheet morphology, but it can be clearly seen that many particles on the surface of the catalyst vanished. Besides, $\mathtt{M n2p}$ peak in the survey spectrum of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ (Fig. S15a) also became weaker, the obviously reduced Mn content further proved that the origination of the leaching manga­ nese ions mainly came from the surface agglomerated manganese ox­ ides. Other changes in $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ were also shown by XRD, XPS, FTIR and Raman. Compared with fresh $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}{\cdot}900$ , the XRD pattern of used $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ (Fig. S16) had no obvious change except for the decrease in peak intensity. The C 1 s peak was almost unchanged, while the O 1 s peak strengthened and N 1 s peak appeared. This demonstrated the adsorption of ABEE and its intermediate products containing oxygen and nitrogen functional groups on the catalyst. In the $\mathtt{M n2p}$ spectrum of the used catalyst (Fig. S15b), the content of $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ decreased from 52.55 $\%$ to $39.74~\%$ and the content of $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathrm{III})$ and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{IV}})$ increased obvi­ ously, indicating the electron transfer from $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ and $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathrm{III})$ to PMS. Additionally, it was worth noting that the ratio of $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ and $\mathtt{C=C}$ in C 1 s (Fig. S15c) and $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=0$ in O 1 s (Fig. S15d) decreased after use, indicating that $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=0$ participated in the catalytic reaction. Meanwhile, the $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value of used $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ decreased to 0.73 (Fig. S17), which meant that the defective sites of the catalyst reduced after the catalytic reac­ tion. Therefore, it can be considered that the decrease in catalytic ac­ tivity might be related to the loss of manganese ions, the adsorption of pollutants and the reduction of defective sites, and it also showed the multiplicity of catalytic mechanisms.
## 3.4. Contributions and synergism effect of manganese oxides and carbon component
To further elucidate the contribution and role of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}$ and carbon component in the activation process of PMS, $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ was etched by $1.0\mathrm{M}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}0_{4}$ solution $\mathtt{80}^{\circ}\mathtt{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ ) for manganese species removal and calcinated in the air at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize8h}$ to eliminate the carbon component, respectively. The obtained $\mathbf{MOF-C_{900}}$ and $\mathbf{MnO_{x}{-}}900$ were respectively used to activate PMS for ABEE degradation. According to the EDS analysis (Fig. S18), the mass ratio of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}{-}}900$ and $\mathbf{MOF-C_{900}}$ should be $48.79:51.21$ . Therefore, the addition amount was roughly in accordance with $49:51$ .
It is worth noting that the ABEE removal efficiency using ${\mathrm{MnO}}_{\mathrm{x}}@{\mathrm{C}}-$ 900/PMS $(91.3\%)$ was smaller than the simple sum of $\mathrm{MnO_{x}{-}}900/\mathrm{PMS}$ $(81.8\%)$ and MOF- ${\bf\cdot}{\bf{C}}_{900}/{\bf\mathrm{PMS}}$ $(72.4\%)$ ), mainly because the degradation of ABEE by PMS could not be ignored. To clarify the synergism between $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathbf{x}}$ and carbon components, the TOC removal ratios in PMS only, $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}{\cdot}900/\mathrm{PMS}$ , $\mathsf{M O F-C}_{900}/\mathsf{P M S}$ , $\begin{array}{r l r}{\mathrm{MnO_{x}\mathrm{-}900}}&{{}+}&{\mathrm{MOF\mathrm{-}C900}/\mathrm{PMS},}\end{array}$ $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C}.900/\mathrm{PMS}$ systems were measured and depicted in Fig. 8b. Their corresponding TOC removal ratios were $2.41\ \%$ , $9.39~\%$ , $18.2\%$ , $24.6~\%$ and $39.46~\%$ , respectively. Additionally, the performance of simply mixed $\mathbf{MnO_{x}{-}}900$ and MOF-C900 was obviously not as good as $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ composites, one reason is that the adsorption of carbon component enriched pollutants on the surface of catalyst [16]. There­ fore, $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathbf{x}}$ and carbon component in $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ composites both had important contributions to PMS activation, and the synergy between them contributed to the higher catalytic activity.
## 3.5. Identification of reactive oxygen species (ROS) in the catalytic process
$g_{0_{4}}\bullet_{\mathbf{\tau}}$ and OH are the most frequently reported active species in PMS activation by metal- or carbon-based materials [60,61]. To find out whether the above ROS exist and their contributions in $\mathrm{MnO}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathrm{C}\cdot900/$ PMS system, classical quenching tests were conducted using methanol (MeOH) and tert-butyl alcohol (TBA) as scavengers. MeOH is known for quenching both $g_{0_{4}}\bullet-$ and •O $\mathrm{~H~}(k_{\mathrm{SO4}\bullet-}=3.2\times10^{6}\mathrm{M}^{-1}s^{-1}$ , $k_{\bullet0\mathrm{H}}=9.7$ $\mathsf{\bar{\times}}10^{8}\mathsf{M}^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ , while TBA can only effectively capture •OH $(k_{\bullet0\mathrm{H}}=3.8$ $\times10^{8}-7.6\times10^{8}\mathrm{M}^{-1}s^{-1})$ [62]. As depicted in Fig. 9a, the inhibitory effects of different concentrations of MeOH on ABEE degradation were very limited, 2 M MeOH caused a $5.4~\%$ drop in removal rate. Inter­ estingly, the addition of TBA induced obvious inhibition on ABEE degradation. As the concentration of TBA increased, ABEE degradation efficiency was reduced by $7.2~\%$ , $14.4~\%$ and $29.9~\%$ , respectively. In order to explore the reasons for the high inhibitory effect of TBA, the adsorption experiments of ABEE in $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}900/\mathrm{TBA}$ system were conducted. As shown in Fig. S20, when the concentration of TBA increased from 0 to $2.0\mathrm{~M~}$ , the adsorption ratio of ABEE obviously dropped from $25.4\%$ to $12.6\%$ . Thus, we inferred that TBA with smaller dielectric constant than methanol could be closer to the surface of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ or be adsorbed by it, thereby influencing the interaction of ABEE and even PMS with the catalyst surface [56,63,64]. Thus, TBA is
Y. Zhao et al.
not reliable to indicate the contribution of $\bullet\mathrm{OH}$ in $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}900/\mathrm{PMS}$ system. This result also suggested that the degradation of ABEE in the $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ system might mainly occur on or near the surface of the catalyst [65].
EPR spectroscopy with DMPO as a probe was used to further ascer­ tain the existence of $g_{0_{4}}\bullet_{\mathbf{\phi}}$ and OH (Fig. 9b). No free radical signals were detected when PMS existed alone. When $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ and PMS were both added, DMPO-OH with the intensity ratio of 1:2:2:1 (hyper­ fine splitting constants of $\upalpha_{\mathrm{N}}=\upalpha_{\mathrm{\upbeta\mathrm{-}H}}=14.9\ \mathrm{G})$ and DMPO- ${\cal S}{\cal O}_{4}^{\mathrm{~-~}}$ with equal intensities (hyperfine splitting constants of $\upalpha_{\mathrm{N}}=13.2\up G$ , $\alpha_{\beta\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}}=9.6$ G, $\upalpha_{\gamma\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H1}}~=~1.48~\mathrm{~G~}$ , $\upalpha_{\gamma\mathrm{-He}}~=~0.78~\mathrm{G}^{\gamma}$ were clearly observed [66]. Furthermore, as the reaction time prolonged, the signal intensity of OH increased significantly, while the intensity of $g_{0_{4}}\bullet_{\mathbf{\phi}}$ was almost un­ changed and always weaker than that of $\bullet\mathrm{OH}$ . There are two possible reasons. Firstly, $\mathrm{DMPO-SO_{4}}^{-}$ was difficult to be sensed by EPR because of its short life and low strength. Secondly, $s0_{4}^{~\bullet-}$ could transfer to OH through a nucleophilic addition with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or $\mathrm{\ooH^{-}}$ [67]. MeOH has been reported to be difficult to access the catalyst surface to eliminate surfacebound radicals [36]. To exclude this possibility, EPR characterization was conducted in $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}900/\mathrm{MeOH}/\mathrm{PMS}$ system [68]. The basically vanished signal of $\mathrm{DMPO-SO_{4}}^{-}$ and DMPO-OH in Fig. S21 indicated that $s0_{4}^{~\bullet-}$ and $\bullet\mathrm{OH}$ had been completely scavenged by MeOH. The above analysis fully proved the existence of $s0_{4}^{~\bullet-}$ and OH and their limited contributions to the degradation of ABEE. It also suggested that $s0_{4}^{~\bullet-}$ and •OH were not the only degradation mechanism, other ROS and electron transfer process may also be involved.
Recently, many studies reported that $^10_{2}$ was the main ROS for carbonaceous materials with oxygen-containing functional groups to activate PMS [21,69,70]. L-Histidine $(k_{102}=3.2\times10^{7}~\mathrm{M}^{-1}{\cdot}s^{-1})$ was employed as an effective quencher to verify the presence and contri­ bution of $^10_{2}$ . Fig. $^{9\mathrm{c}}$ showed that L-Histidine could notably inhibit ABEE degradation. When the dosage of L-Histidine increased from $\mathsf{5m M}$ to $30\mathrm{mM}$ , the removal ratio of ABEE decreased continuously and finally stabilized at $29.4~\%$ , suggesting that $^10_{2}$ should be the main ROS in $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C}.900/\mathrm{PMS}$ system. 2,2,6,6-tetramethyl-4-piperidone (TEMP) was selected as a probe in EPR characterization to further examine the existence of $^10_{2}$ , the generated 2,2,6,6-tetramethylpiperidine-1-oxyl (TEMPO) can be recognized as a triplet signal with equal intensities [71]. As presented in Fig. 9d, the weak signal was observed when PMS existed alone, which was due to the self-decomposition of PMS (Eq. (14)) [72]. The strength of TEMPO peaks increased obviously upon the introduction of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ , and the signal became stronger as reac­ tion time prolonged from 1 min to $10\mathrm{min}$ , confirming the promotion of $^10_{2}$ production. These results proved that $^10_{2}$ played a vital role in ABEE degradation. The superoxide radical $(\mathsf{O}_{2}^{\bullet-})$ has been reported as an important intermediate species in the generation process of $^10_{2}$ [24,73]. Hence, superoxide dismutase (SOD) was introduced to the $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}{\cdot}900$ system to quench $0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ (Fig. S22a), the degradation of ABEE was inhibited to a certain extent $(7.1\ \%)$ . The weak signal of $\mathrm{DMPO-O_{2}^{\bullet-}}$ in Fig. S22b also proved its limited contribution to the origination of $^10_{2}$ .
