## 3.6. Surface electron transfer mechanism
In addition to ROS, surface electron transfer process is also frequently observed in carbonaceous materials [77,78]. In view of the superior synergistic effect of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathbf{x}}$ and carbon component in $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C-}$ 900 composites, it is rational to believe that the existence of $\mathtt{M n O_{x}}$ could promote this process. Herein, electrochemical measurements including electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and linear sweep vol­ tammetry (LSV) were conducted to verify the above inferences.
EIS measurements for $\mathbf{MOF-C_{900}}$ and $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}500/700/900/1000$ composites were first performed to explore the differences in chargetransfer resistance of these catalysts. As seen from Fig. 10a, all cata­ lysts presented well-defined semicircles, and $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ presented the smallest diameter, suggesting that $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ manifested smaller electron transfer resistance and faster electron transfer capability [79,80]. The order of EIS values of $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C}$ composites corresponded well with the sequence of calcination temperature in the range of $500^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to $900^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , which was because the temperature could affect the carbonization degree and thus affect the surface electro-conductivity. However, the increased EIS value of $\mathtt{M n O_{x}}@\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-}1000$ indicated that graphitization degree was not the only influencing factor, the surface area and micropore volume of catalysts could also affect this process [81]. Moreover, it is worth noting that the Nyquist hemicycle radius of $\mathbf{MOF-C_{900}}$ was significantly larger than that of other $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C}$ com­ posites. Hence, it is apparent that the existence of $\mathtt{M n O_{x}}$ could facilitate the interfacial electron transfer.
The LSV curves of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ , $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900.$ /ABEE, ${\mathrm{MnO}}_{\mathrm{x}}@{\mathrm{C}}-$ 900/PMS and $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C}\cdot900/\mathrm{PMS}/\mathrm{\Lambda}$ ABEE systems were recorded in Fig. 10b to demonstrate the electron transfer mechanism. In the LSV method, as the applied voltage is positively shifted, the Fermi level $(\mathrm{E_{F}})$ of the substance in solution will continue to decrease [82]. When the $\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{F}}$ is lower than the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) energy level of the substance, electrons will be transferred from the substance to the electrode, an oxidation reaction occurs. In addition, the current density will increase as the voltage continues to increase. When the supply of substance on the electrode surface tends to stabilize or decrease, the current density remains unchanged or declines, resulting in an oxidation peak. Therefore, the voltage corresponding to the oxidation peak will change with the variation of the electrode reaction system. When only $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ existed, the oxidation peak should correspond to the oxidation of water. The addition of ABEE or PMS both produced a higher density current and positively shifted oxidation peak, which should be due to the electron transfer between ABEE or PMS and the surface of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ . When ABEE and PMS were both injected into the electrolyte, another significant increase of current was trig­ gered, proving that a faster electron flow was formed on the surface of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ from the pollutant to PMS [83]. This result confirmed the surface electron transfer mechanism in $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C}\cdot900/\mathrm{PI}$ MS/ABEE sys­ tem. Besides, it should be noted that the establishment of the ternary system mainly depended on the surface affinity of the catalyst to ABEE and PMS. That is to say, the adsorption of ABEE on $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ and the metastable reaction complex formed by the carbon defectives of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ and PMS played essential roles in electron transfer pro­ cess [83].
## 3.7. Degradation pathways of ABEE
Twenty-two intermediates of ABEE degradation by $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ activated PMS were identified by LC-MS (Fig. S23), four of them were reported in AOPs for the first time. The formulas and corresponding structures of these intermediates were summarized in Table. S4. Accordingly, four possible degradation pathways were presented in Fig. 11. In pathway A (PA), ABEE was firstly attacked by $g_{0_{4}}\bullet_{\mathbf{\phi}}$ through electron transfer process, generating a $_{\mathrm{HO}_{3}S O}$ – substitution product (PA-1) [84]. Then, PA-1 underwent oxidation of $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}$ group and breaking of C–C bond to form $\ N O_{2^{-}}$ group (PA-5) and carboxylic acid (PA-6). Before these oxidation reactions, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -group of PA-1 could also be replaced by the –OH group to form PA-2 and subsequent prod­ ucts (PA-3 and PA-4). Besides, OH alone could lead to pathway B (PB). PB-1 was formed through $-\mathrm{OH}$ substitution, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}$ group, C–C/C-N bond were attacked by •OH to generate corresponding carboxylic acid (PB-2, PB-3, PB-4 and PB-5). Pathway C (PC) showed that neither $s0_{4}^{~\bullet-}$ nor OH was involved, but direct hydrogen abstraction reaction [38]. In pathway D, –OH substitution process and diazotization of $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ group could produce PD-1 and dihydroxy (PD-2), and PD-2 was further oxidized to PD-3. The above intermediates might combine with each other to generate larger molecules (P’-1, P’-2 and ${\bf\vec{P}}^{'}{\bf-}3_{.}^{\cdot}$ ), these larger molecules could be oxidized into low molecular weight products $(\mathbf{P}^{'}\cdot1$ and P’’-2) by ROS in $\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@\mathrm{C}.900/\mathrm{PMS}$ system and finally completely mineralized [2].
## 3.8. Activation mechanisms of PMS over MnOx@C-900
Based on the above results and previous studies, a possible mecha­ nism of PMS activation for ABEE degradation was proposed (Fig. 12). As displayed, electron transfer and ROS including $^10_{2},\ 0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ , •OH and $g_{0_{4}}\bullet_{\mathbf{\phi}}$ all participated in the PMS activation process. Firstly, OH, $s0_{4}^{~\bullet-}$ and $0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ could be produced through the redox reaction between the surface $\mathrm{Mn^{II}/M n^{I I I}/\bar{M}n^{I V}}$ species and PMS (Eqs. (15) – (17)) and Eq. (20)). Among them, part of $\bullet\mathrm{OH}$ could be converted from $g_{0_{4}}\bullet_{\mathbf{\Phi}}$ (Eqs. (18) – (19)). Secondly, the self-decomposition of PMS (Eq. (14)) and the recombination of $\boldsymbol{0_{2}}^{\bullet-}$ (Eq. (21)) produced a small part of $^1{\cal O}_{2},$ $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ groups (Eqs. (22)–(25)) of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ played a vital role in $^10_{2}$ production. Thirdly, $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ could act as an electron shuttle to mediate the electron transfer between PMS and ABEE, realizing the direct oxidation of ABEE. Meanwhile, the carbon component of $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ with abundant $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=0$ groups and defective sites, as well as rational $\ensuremath{S_{\mathrm{BET}}}$ and pore volume could enrich ABEE and PMS molecules on the surface of the catalyst, which was conducive to the PMS activation by surface manganese species and ABEE degradation by the generated ROS. Additionally, $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ in $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ composites effectively improved its electron transfer capability, thus accelerating the degra­ dation of ABEE. Therefore, the joint effect of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathbf{x}}$ and carbon component in $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ was the key to its excellent catalytic performance.
## 4. Conclusions
In this work, a series of novel nanosheet structured $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C}$ com­ posites obtained by in-situ calcining 2D Mn-MOFs under different tem­ peratures (Ar) was utilized as a PMS activator to degrade ABEE for the first time. The results demonstrated that the pyrolysis temperature could affect the structure of the carbonaceous substrate and the valence states of Mn species. Benefiting from the rational coordination of specific surface area and pore volume, higher $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ content, more carbonyl groups and defective sites, $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ exhibited excellent adsorption and degradation performance for ABEE. Free radicals and non-radical pathways including $s0_{4}^{~\bullet-}$ , •OH, $^10_{2}$ , ${\mathsf{O}}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ and surface electron transfer process were all proven to be involved in the $\mathtt{M n O_{x}@C-900/\xi}$ PMS/ABEE system. Among them, $^10_{2}$ played a leading role in elimi­ nating ABEE. Moreover, the synergistic effect of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ and carbon component could not be ignored. The carbon component could be complexed with PMS and adsorb ABEE on the catalyst surface, which could facilitate the reaction between manganese oxides and PMS, as well as the generated ROS and ABEE. Besides, $\mathtt{M n O_{x}}$ in $\mathbf{MnO_{x}}@\mathbf{C}\cdot900$ could also improve the electron transfer capability of the carbon component. This study provided useful guidance for the simple synthesis of manganese-carbon composites with $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ evenly embedded in the carbonaceous substrate. The in-depth understanding of manganesecarbon interaction mechanisms in PMS activation might prompt the development of other MOFs-derived metal–carbon hybrid materials for environmental remediation.
